**Population genetics — 23.3.1 Q1–Q3**

The revised PRS calculation correctly joins the beta for each locus, multiplies it by genotype dosage and sums by donor. There are 300 unique donors and 15 loci, with no missing genotypes or unmatched locus weights.

The highest score is d249 (control), 2.389; the lowest is d205 (case), 0.172.

| Group | n | Minimum | Maximum | Mean | Median | SD |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Cases | 150 | 0.172 | 2.247 | 1.3120 | 1.3225 | 0.4203 |
| Controls | 150 | 0.192 | 2.389 | 1.0452 | 1.0245 | 0.4016 |

Your statement that both distributions are symmetric should be revised. Cases are approximately symmetric; controls show positive skew. Both groups overlap considerably. The two low case outliers (d190, d205) and two high control outliers (d249, d266) are reproduced using the standard boxplot rule.

The Shapiro–Wilk p-values are 0.2129 for cases and 0.007452 for controls. A non-significant result does not confirm normality. Do not write that Shapiro–Wilk will “confirm” normality in both groups, or that a significant control result automatically rules out every t-test: with 150 participants per group, Welch’s test can remain appropriate for comparing means. The chosen Wilcoxon rank-sum test is a reasonable rank-based comparison and gives W = 15554, p = 1.013 × 10⁻⁸.

Add that each donor contributes one PRS, the groups contain different donors, and independence between donors is a sampling-design assumption. With unequal distribution shapes, interpret the Wilcoxon result as evidence of a distribution/rank difference, not solely a test of medians. Correct “Pakinson” to “Parkinson’s”.

