**RNA-seq ontology — 23.3.2 Q2**

The two annotation groups for the actual significant set are:

| GO ID | Supplied description | Number of DE genes | Direction |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| GO:0006979 | Response to oxidative stress | 21 | Upregulated |
| GO:0007268 | Synaptic transmission | 18 | Downregulated |

The category names/counts happen to match your named top-39 output because both the omitted significant gene and the added non-significant OXR1 fall in the oxidative-stress category. That coincidence does not make the gene selection correct.

Use the full FDR-defined set and join annotations by Entrez ID. Do not remove a significant result just because its symbol is missing. Selecting 39 annotation rows after a join is also fragile: gene-to-GO joins can have more than one row per gene, so a fixed row limit can truncate genes or terms even though that is not the main problem in this particular dataset.

For example, using your existing `top_tibble` and learned functions:

```r
DE_results <- top_tibble |>
  filter(adj.P.Val < 0.05) |>
  mutate(entrezgene_id = as.numeric(as.character(genes))) |>
  left_join(entrez_genename_key,
            by = join_by(entrezgene_id == entrezid)) |>
  left_join(gene_ontology, by = join_by(entrezgene_id))

DE_results |>
  dplyr::select(entrezgene_id, gene_name, logFC, adj.P.Val, go_id, desc)
```

This identifies GO memberships. It is not a formal GO enrichment test, so describe the categories as annotations rather than statistically “enriched” pathways unless an enrichment analysis is separately performed.

**RNA-seq clustering — 23.3.2 Q3**

Cases 1–5 form a group separate from the eight controls, while cases 6–8 cluster with the controls. Thus, gene-expression clustering is partly, not completely, consistent with disease status. Your current paragraph says both that controls are to the right and that three cases are to the left “where the controls are”; fix that contradiction. It is safer to describe which samples cluster together because the sign/orientation of an MDS axis can flip without changing the result.

Clustering alone does not establish a batch effect or sample mislabelling. Biological differences, including the PRS pattern identified in linking Q2, are another explanation. Present batch effects and mislabelling as possibilities to investigate, not demonstrated causes.

