**Clinical gait summary — 23.3.4 Q1**

The numerical descriptions are reproduced: participant means range from −0.9136° to 0.7764°, medians from −1.4046° to 0.4925°, SDs from 2.3465° to 17.2599°, and individual observations from −50.1667° to 60.8307°. Mean absolute deviations range from 1.8841° to 13.8246°; d056 has the highest value.

Your calculation `mean(abs(gait))` is sensible for summarising average distance from upright without cancellation between positive and negative tilts. Call it “mean absolute deviation from upright (°)”. The paragraph should not call it a direct measure of both balance and temporal smoothness and then later say it does not measure smoothness. It is a balance/posture proxy; temporal smoothness depends on the order and changes between measurements.

**Clinical treatment comparison — 23.3.4 Q2**

The treatment means (L-DOPA 8.762259°, new compound 5.699946°), group normality p-values (0.1284, 0.5617) and Welch test p = 0.0350281 are correct. There are eight participants per treatment, and four participants in each treatment-by-exercise combination.

The estimated unadjusted difference is −3.062314° for new compound versus L-DOPA. Explain that each patient, not each of their 700 measurements, is the unit of analysis. Equal variances are not required by the Welch test you used. The normality checks show no detected departure, rather than confirming normality.

**Clinical age/exercise adjustment — 23.3.4 Q3**

The separate age and exercise models are reproduced: age β = 0.272859 (p = 0.003218) and exercise β = −3.096132 (p = 0.032108). Label these as unadjusted associations.

In the final model `absolute_mean ~ treatment + age + exercise`, the new-compound coefficient is **−2.692217°**, not +2.6922°. Your verbal statement “2.6922° lower” is correct; the sign in parentheses is wrong. Its p-value is 0.0001234 and its 95% confidence interval is approximately −3.7469° to −1.6376°.

In that same adjusted model, age β = 0.235889 (p = 0.00001316) and exercise β = −2.791893 (p = 0.00008758). Do not mix these adjusted coefficients with the separate-model estimates without explaining the distinction.

Check the residuals of this specific adjusted model as well. The diagnostics of the later PRS models do not automatically validate it. The recalculated normality, variance and RESET p-values for this model are approximately 0.9932, 0.7130 and 0.5414, respectively; these do not detect a violation but do not prove all assumptions.

